<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manchukuo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">from <a href="/years/1932">1932</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manchukuo was a puppet state established by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> in 1932, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mukden-incident">Mukden Incident</a> and the Japanese occupation of the region. It was nominally headed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puyi">Puyi</a>, the last Qing emperor. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> exploited its resources and industry until the state collapsed in 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manchukuo.jpg" alt="Manchukuo" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Urmas · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Manchukuo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, now Northeast China, was historically the homeland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> people, but by the twentieth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> were by far its largest group. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> fell in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinhai-revolution">Xinhai Revolution</a> of 1911 and the last emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puyi">Puyi</a> abdicated at the age of six. In the Warlord Era Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-zuolin">Zhang Zuolin</a> established himself in Manchuria with Japanese backing, and the Japanese Kwantung Army had him assassinated in 1928, expecting anarchy that would justify seizing the region. The plot failed because his son Zhang Xueliang succeeded him without incident.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> invaded and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> in 1931 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenyang">Mukden</a> incident. A puppet government was set up the following year, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puyi">Puyi</a> as nominal regent. Manchukuo was proclaimed a monarchy on 1 March 1934, with Puyi as emperor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The State Council was the centre of power. Each of its ministers was assisted by a Japanese vice-minister, and the commander-in-chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwantung-army">Kwantung Army</a> served as Japanese ambassador with the ability to veto the emperor's decisions. Chinese advisers gradually resigned or were dismissed. The Legislative Council was largely ceremonial, and the only authorised party was the government-sponsored Concordia Association. Manchukuo received only limited diplomatic recognition, mainly from states aligned with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis powers</a>.</p><p>More than a million Japanese settled there by 1945, many of them land-poor farmers resettled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Under the vice-minister Nobusuke Kishi and the Manchurian Industrial Development Company heavy industry expanded, using the forced labour of local people. Manchukuo was the base for Japan's invasion of the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, beginning with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugou-bridge">Marco Polo Bridge</a> incident of 1937.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>On 8 August 1945 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and invaded Manchukuo. Its imperial army, on paper 200,000 strong, performed poorly, and whole units surrendered without firing a shot. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puyi">Puyi</a> abdicated on 17 August. The Soviets captured him and later extradited him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, where he was imprisoned on war crimes charges after the Communist Party came to power in 1949. The government was formally dissolved after Japan's surrender in September 1945, and the territory was transferred to Chinese administration the following year.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manchukuo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The colonies were assigned ranked roles in the empire's economy: Taiwan supplied sugar and rice; Korea provided rice, labour, and later industrial output; <b>Manchukuo</b> after 1932 supplied coal, iron, and strategic minerals essential for military production.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Korea also became a major exporter of opium and narcotics in the 1930s, supplying the opium monopoly of <b>Manchukuo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/korea-under-japanese-rule">Korea under Japanese rule · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
